# Ledgerkit CLI usage

Ledgerkit is invoked as `python -m ledgerkit <command> [options] [journal...]`.
The package's declared version is `1.0.0.dev1`, it requires Python 3.8 or
later, and it declares zero required third-party runtime dependencies (an
optional `pandas` extra exists for the `.to_dataframe()` convenience methods
on report results) (current-state@v1#current-state-001).

## Command synopsis

The CLI exposes exactly six subcommands, selected via a single required
positional `command` argument: `balance`, `register`, `accounts`, `print`,
`stats`, `check`. There is no per-subcommand argument parser — all six
commands share one flat set of global flags (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-001).
The top-level `--help` output is:

```
usage: ledgerkit [-h] [--version] [-v] [-1] [-o FILE] [-f FILE] [-s] [-I]
                 [-q TERMS] [-c STYLE]
                 {balance,register,accounts,print,stats,check} [journal ...]

Plain-text accounting (hledger-compatible)

positional arguments:
  {balance,register,accounts,print,stats,check}
                        Report to run: balance, register, accounts, print,
                        stats, check
  journal               Journal file (shorthand for -f; all commands except
                        check), or check names (check command only)

options:
  -h, --help            show this help message and exit
  --version             show program's version number and exit
  -v, --verbose         Show more detailed output (stats: commodity names)
  -1                    Show a single tab-separated line of output (stats
                        only)
  -o, --output-file FILE
                        Write output to FILE instead of stdout
  -f, --file FILE       Read data from FILE, or stdin if -. May be specified
                        more than once.
  -s, --strict          Enable strict mode: also check that accounts and
                        commodities are declared
  -I, --ignore-assertions
                        Disable balance assertion checking
  -q, --query TERMS     Filter using a query string (see ledgerkit.query),
                        e.g. 'acct:food date:2024'. Applies to balance,
                        register, accounts, stats, and print.
  -c, --commodity-style STYLE
                        Override display style for a commodity, e.g.
                        '$1,000.00' or '1.000,00 EUR'. May be specified more
                        than once.
```

(current-state@v1#current-state-004)

Running `--help` on any individual subcommand (e.g. `python -m ledgerkit
balance --help` or `python -m ledgerkit check --help`) prints this exact
same top-level usage/help block, word for word — there is no
subcommand-specific help page, because there is no subcommand-level parser
to attach one to (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-001,
current-state@v1#current-state-005).

## Global options

These flags apply uniformly to every command — they are defined once on the
single top-level parser, not scoped per-subcommand
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-002):

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `--version` | Show the program's version number and exit |
| `-v`, `--verbose` | Show more detailed output (for `stats`: also list commodity names) |
| `-1` | Show a single tab-separated line of output (`stats` only) |
| `-o`, `--output-file FILE` | Write output to `FILE` instead of stdout |
| `-f`, `--file FILE` | Read data from `FILE`, or stdin if `-`. May be given more than once. |
| `-s`, `--strict` | Enable strict mode: additionally check that accounts and commodities are declared |
| `-I`, `--ignore-assertions` | Disable balance-assertion checking |
| `-q`, `--query TERMS` | Filter using a query string (e.g. `'acct:food date:2024'`). Applies to `balance`, `register`, `accounts`, `stats`, and `print`. |
| `-c`, `--commodity-style STYLE` | Override the display style for a commodity, e.g. `'$1,000.00'` or `'1.000,00 EUR'`. May be given more than once. |

(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-002)

## Specifying the journal file

For every command **except `check`**, the journal file is resolved with
strict precedence: (1) one or more `-f`/`--file` flags; (2) a single
positional `journal` argument, as a shorthand for `-f`; (3) the
`$LEDGER_FILE` environment variable; (4) `~/.hledger.journal`, if it exists.
Combining `-f` with a positional argument is a hard error (exit code 1,
"cannot combine -f/--file flags with a positional journal argument"). If
none of the four sources resolve to a file, the CLI exits 1 with "no journal
file specified; use -f FILE or set $LEDGER_FILE"
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-003).

**`check` is the one exception to this rule.** For `check`, every positional
argument is treated as the *name* of a check to run, never as a journal
file — even though the same positional slot is a file-path shorthand for
every other command. The journal must still be supplied to `check` via
`-f`, `$LEDGER_FILE`, or the default `~/.hledger.journal` file
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-003). Concretely, `ledgerkit check
sample.journal` (with no `-f`) does **not** check `sample.journal` — it is
read as an (unrecognised) check name, and because no `-f`/`$LEDGER_FILE`
was given either, the command fails with "no journal file specified; use -f
FILE or set $LEDGER_FILE" (current-state@v1#current-state-008). The correct
form is `ledgerkit check -f sample.journal [check-name ...]`.

## `balance`

`balance` prints one line per (account, commodity) pair that has a nonzero
net amount, with the formatted amount right-aligned first and the account
name second — the account name is printed only on the last line for that
account, after all of its commodity lines. A dashed separator line follows,
sized to the width of the widest amount/total string, and then one line per
nonzero commodity grand total, or a single bare `"0"` if every commodity
nets to zero overall. Negative amounts are wrapped in ANSI red escape codes
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-005).

Ordinary zero-net (account, commodity) rows are omitted from the output —
**except** a depth-0 `"..."` row (produced by a `depth:0` query), which is
always printed unconditionally regardless of its own value. In an
unfiltered, fully-balanced journal this collapsed row happens to net to
zero, but the rule is not "shown because it's zero" — combined with an
account-selecting `-q` filter, the same depth-0 `"..."` row can net to a
nonzero amount (the filtered subset need not itself balance) and is still
shown, under the same unconditional rule (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-005).

## `register`

`register` prints a chronological list, one line per posting, in a
fixed-width format: a 10-character date column, a 21-character description
column, a 22-character (abbreviated) account column, a right-aligned
12-character amount column, and a right-aligned 13-character running-balance
column. The date and description columns are left blank on every row after
the first posting of a given (date, description) pair, so only the first
posting of each transaction shows its date and description
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-007).

Account names longer than 20 characters are abbreviated for this column by
progressively shortening their leading (non-last) colon-separated
components — first to 2 characters, then to 1, if still too wide — so
`equity:opening-balances` renders as `eq:opening-balances`. A running
balance of exactly zero is shown as a bare `"0"` with no commodity symbol
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-007).

The underlying running balance is a single cumulative sum of each posting's
amount across every row, in output order, and is documented and tested as
never being re-grouped or re-aggregated per account — even when a `depth:`
query clips several postings' displayed account names down to the same
string, each posting still contributes its own row and its own increment to
the one running total (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-006). This specific
interaction between `depth:` clipping and the running-balance column has
not been independently re-verified beyond the project's own documentation
and test suite, and should be treated with that caveat.

## `accounts`

`accounts` prints the sorted, deduplicated list of account names, one per
line, with no separator and no zero-total convention. This means a `-q`
query that matches nothing produces genuinely empty output — unlike
`balance`, which always prints a separator and a total line even when
nothing matches (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-008).

## `print`

`print` prints each matching transaction as a header line of the form
`DATE flag DESCRIPTION` — where `flag` is `" * "` for a cleared transaction,
`" ! "` for a pending one, and a single space otherwise — followed by one
indented line per posting (four spaces of indent, the account name
left-padded to 40 characters, then the formatted amount, or just the
account name on its own if the posting is elided), then a blank line
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-009).

A `-q`/`--query` filter on `print` matches at the whole-transaction level:
if *any* posting in a transaction matches, the *entire* transaction — every
one of its postings, not only the matching one — is printed. `print` also
deliberately ignores `depth:` entirely: it neither filters postings by
depth nor clips account names to a depth limit, matching hledger's own
`print` behaviour (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-009).

`print` does not include inline comments in its output. Neither a
transaction-header-level comment nor a posting-level comment is ever
printed by `print`, even though Ledgerkit's own journal serialiser
(`transaction_to_text`/`journal_to_text`, used for round-tripping a
`Transaction`/`Journal` back to journal-format text) does emit those same
comments. This is a confirmed, reproducible fact about `print`'s current
output — whether it reflects a deliberate scope decision for the `print`
command specifically, or an unnoted gap relative to the serialiser's own
round-trip guarantee, is not established by the available evidence and is
not resolved here (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-010).

Separately, Ledgerkit's `journal_to_text()` serialiser is documented as
round-tripping a transaction's date, flag, description, postings, amounts,
and comments, and as explicitly not serialising directives (`account`,
`commodity`, `payee`, `P`) — callers needing full round-trip fidelity for
directives are expected to work from the raw source file instead
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-014). This directive-exclusion detail is
documented and tested at the serialiser level but has a narrower evidence
base than the rest of this section, and should be read with that in mind.

## `stats`

`stats` reports deterministic journal statistics — transaction count, date
range, transactions-per-day figures, payee count, account count and depth,
commodity count and names, and price count. Runtime figures (elapsed time,
transactions per second) are measured and formatted only by the CLI itself,
not stored alongside the deterministic data (reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-011).

By default, `stats` prints a multi-line labelled report. A sample run
against a small journal produces output of this shape:

```
Main file           : /path/to/sample.journal
Included files      : 0
Txns span           : 2024-01-01 to 2024-01-20 (20 days)
Last txn            : 2024-01-20 (987 days ago)
Txns                : 5 (0.2 per day)
Txns last 30 days   : 0 (0.0 per day)
Txns last 7 days    : 0 (0.0 per day)
Payees/descriptions : 5
Accounts            : 6 (depth 3)
Commodities         : 1
Market prices       : 0
Runtime stats       : 0.02 s elapsed, 211 txns/s
```

(current-state@v1#current-state-008)

With `-1`, `stats` instead prints a single tab-separated line:
`VERSION`, the source file path (or `"(none)"`), the elapsed-time string,
and the transactions-per-second string. With `-v`/`--verbose`, the
"Commodities" line additionally lists the commodity names in parentheses
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-011).

`stats` is the one report command where a `depth:N` query **excludes**
accounts deeper than the limit from the account count and account-depth
figures, rather than clipping and rolling them up the way `balance`,
`register`, and `accounts` all do for the same `depth:` query. This is
documented as a genuine, deliberate exception mirroring a corresponding
behaviour in hledger itself, not an oversight specific to this command
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-012).

## `check`

`check` is the only command with a distinct control-flow path: it runs
before output is redirected to any `-o` file, and on success it prints
**nothing at all** to stdout, exiting 0. This silent success is the single
most important thing to know about `check`'s behaviour — there is no "all
checks passed" message, no count of checks run, nothing written anywhere
on a clean journal. On any check failure, `check` prints each failing
check's message to stderr and exits 1
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-015, current-state@v1#current-state-008).

Positional arguments to `check` name the specific checks to run, in
addition to the checks that always run by default (see below) — see
"Specifying the journal file" above for why a bare positional given to
`check` is never treated as a journal path.

### The default/strict/named check structure

Validation checks are organised into three fixed tiers:

- **Basic** — `parseable`, `autobalanced`, `assertions` — run on *every*
  CLI command by default, not just on `check`. This means any command
  (`balance`, `stats`, `print`, etc.) will fail outright if the journal
  has, for example, a failing balance assertion, unless `-I` is passed.
- **Strict** — `accounts`, `commodities` — run in addition to the basic
  tier only when `-s`/`--strict` is given, for *any* command.
- **Other** — `payees`, `ordereddates`, `uniqueleafnames` — run only when
  explicitly named as a positional argument to the `check` command.

(checks@v1#checks-001)

`-I`/`--ignore-assertions` suppresses only the `assertions` check from the
basic tier; it is applied both to the default basic-check gate that runs
before every command and to `check` itself when `check` is the command
being run. `-s`/`--strict` additionally runs the strict-tier checks for
every non-`check` command, and is forwarded into `check` as well
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-015).

### What each named check does

- **`autobalanced`** treats a transaction as balanced if it has exactly one
  posting with no explicit amount ("elided"), regardless of how many
  commodities appear among its other postings. A transaction with two or
  more elided postings fails unconditionally. A transaction with no elided
  postings must have every commodity's postings net to exactly zero; any
  commodity that doesn't produces its own separate error
  (checks@v1#checks-002).
- **`accounts`** (strict tier) scans transactions and postings in order and
  stops at the very first posting whose account has not been declared via
  an `account` directive, comparing names case-sensitively. If no accounts
  have been declared at all, the very first posting's account triggers
  this single error (checks@v1#checks-003).
- **`commodities`** (strict tier) likewise stops at the first posting whose
  commodity symbol has not been declared, skipping any posting with no
  amount at all. A posting whose amount's commodity symbol is the empty
  string (a bare, unit-less zero amount) is always exempt from this check,
  even if the empty string itself has not been declared
  (checks@v1#checks-004).
- Failures from the **strict** tier (`accounts`, `commodities`) produce a
  distinctive, multi-line error message — an `Error: file:line:` header, a
  reconstructed view of the offending transaction and posting lines, a
  caret (`^`) underline positioned under the offending account name or
  amount, an explanation of why strict checking flagged it, and an example
  `account`/`commodity` directive the user could add to fix it. Every other
  check's error message, by contrast, is a single plain-text line
  (checks@v1#checks-005).
- **`assertions`** (basic tier) validates balance assertions written with
  `=`, `==`, `=*`, and `==*` syntax — `=`/`==` check only the named account
  itself, `=*`/`==*` additionally sum in every subaccount; `==`/`==*`
  additionally require every other commodity present in that account (or
  subtree) to be exactly zero. Postings are processed in `(date,
  source-line)` order, not journal/parse order, while maintaining a running
  per-account, per-commodity balance. An expected/actual mismatch produces
  one error, and a sole-commodity violation (a nonzero "other" commodity
  found where none was expected) produces its own, separate error
  (checks@v1#checks-006).
- **`payees`** (other tier) flags each distinct transaction description
  that is not present among the journal's declared payees, reporting each
  distinct offending description only once regardless of how many
  transactions repeat it; if no payees have been declared, every
  description is flagged. **`ordereddates`** (other tier) flags any
  transaction whose date is earlier than a running "high-water mark" — the
  most recent date that itself passed the check — which is *not*
  necessarily the same as the date of the literal immediately preceding
  transaction: once a transaction is flagged as out of order, the
  high-water mark is left unchanged, so it continues to be the baseline
  for the next comparison too. Transactions sharing the same date never
  trigger this check. **`uniqueleafnames`** (other tier) flags every
  colon-separated leaf name (the text after an account name's last `:`,
  or the whole name if there is no `:`) that is shared by two or more
  distinct full account names anywhere in the journal
  (checks@v1#checks-007).

### A caveat on combining `-s`/`-I` with explicit check names

`run_checks` (the function backing both the always-on basic-check gate and
the `check` command's own named-check pass) always runs the basic tier
minus anything in its `skip` set, then — if strict mode is on — the strict
tier, and finally any explicitly named checks not already covered by one
of those tiers. The bookkeeping that decides whether an explicitly named
check has "already" run is based on static tier membership, not on which
checks actually executed. One documented, confirmed consequence: naming a
basic-tier check explicitly while that same check is also being skipped
(e.g. explicitly requesting `assertions` while also passing
`-I`/`--ignore-assertions`) causes that check to run **zero** times rather
than being deduplicated down to exactly once, because it is still counted
as "already run" by tier membership even though the skip suppressed its
actual execution. A related, separately confirmed asymmetry is that
`-s`/`--strict`'s own checks are not filtered by `skip` at all, so a
strict-tier check named in `skip` still runs whenever strict mode is on.
Whether either of these two interactions is an intentional design choice
or an unintended gap is not established by the available evidence and is
left open here rather than guessed at (checks@v1#checks-008). This entire
caveat rests on evidence graded only as partially supported, and should be
treated as the least certain claim in this document.

## Exit codes

A successful `check` run, and a successful `accounts`/`stats` run, exit 0
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-008, current-state@v1#current-state-008). A
`check` failure, a failing basic check on any command (unless suppressed
with `-I`), a file-resolution error (no journal found, or `-f` combined
with a positional journal argument), and a `check` command whose bare
positional was consumed as a check name instead of a file (see
"Specifying the journal file" above) all exit 1
(reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-003, reports-cli@v1#reports-cli-015,
current-state@v1#current-state-008).
